<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day19" xml:id="day-19-lecture">
  <title>Day 19</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Ch 6.3: PCR
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <title>Covered in this class</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Why do you want to use the PCs in regression models?
          <ul>
            <li>
              What assumptions do you have to make for it to be a good idea to
              use <em>principal component regression (PCR)</em>?
            </li>

            <li>
              Conversely, what is a typical bad scenario in which to use PCR?
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          How do you implement PCR in Python?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          How do you interpret the model coefficients when using PCR?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          How do you choose the number of PCs to use in PCR?
          <ul>
            <li>
              Given a figure of, for example, cross-validation score as a
              function of the number of PCs, you should be able to choose
              appropriately and provide rationales in terms of the bias-variance
              tradeoff.
            </li>

            <li>You should be able to generate such figures in Python.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          What is the relationship between PCR and the feature selection and
          regularization methods that you learned in this part of the course?
          What are the differences?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Principal Components Regression</title>

    <p>
      So you've found your PCA coefficients
    </p>

    <p>
      Original predictors:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        X_1,\cdots,X_p
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      New predictors:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        Z_1,\cdots,Z_M
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        <mrow> Z_m \amp= \sum_{j=1}^{p}\phi_{jm}X_j=X_1 \phi_{1m}+X_2 \phi_{2m}+\ldots+X_p \phi_{pm}</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \begin{bmatrix}X_1 \amp X_2 \amp \ldots \amp X_p\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\phi_{1m}\\\phi_{2m}\\ \vdots\\\phi_{pm} \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        Z_m = \sum_{j=1}^{p}\phi_{jm}X_j
      </md>
    </p>
    <!-- <p>Learned model:</p>
          <p>
            <md>
              y = \theta_0 + \theta_1 Z_1 + \cdots + \theta_M Z_M
            </md>
          </p> -->
    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          Now what?
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Do linear regression on <m>Z_1,\ldots,Z_M</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The book calls this principal components regression.
                Other names include
                <ul>
                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Orthogonal regression.
                    </p>
                  </li>

                  <li>
                    <p>
                      Total least squares.
                    </p>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The linear model is
                <md>
                  y = \theta_0 + \theta_1 Z_1 + \cdots + \theta_M Z_M.
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <p>
          What are we assuming?
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The directions in which <m>X_1,\ldots,X_p</m> have the most
              variation are the directions that are associated with <m>Y</m>.
            </li>

            <li>
              If this assumption holds, fitting on <m>Z_i</m> is better than
              fitting on <m>X_i</m> since using fewer variables lessens the
              chance of overfitting.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>

    <note>
      <title>Interpreting the coefficients of <m>Z_i</m></title>

      <p>
        The coefficients of <m>Z_i</m> cannot be used directly to interpret the
        effects of <m>X_i</m>.
      </p>
    </note>

    <example>
      <title>Interpreting coefficients of <m>Z_i</m></title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          Assume that the loading matrix is given by
          <md>
            \phi = \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp -1 \\ 
        3 \amp \frac{1}{2} \\ 
        0 \amp \frac{1}{2}
        \end{bmatrix}
          </md>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What are the values for <m>p</m> and <m>M</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p>
            What are the dimensions of <m>\phi</m>?
            One of them is <m>p</m>, and the other is <m>M</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>

        <answer>
          <p>
            The dimension of <m>\phi</m> is <m>p\times M=3 \times 2</m>, so
            <m>p=3</m> and <m>M=2</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="day19-PCR-original-coeffs">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Assume the trained model is given by <m>y=2 Z_1 - Z_2</m>.
            Write down this model in terms of the <m>p</m> original predictors.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p>
            The linear transformation from the <m>X</m> s to <m>Z_m</m> is given
            by
            <md>
              Z_m = \sum_{j=1}^{p}\phi_{jm}X_j.
            </md>
          </p>
        </hint>

        <answer>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow>y \amp= 2(X_1+3X_2+0X_3)-(-X_1+\tfrac{1}{2}X_2+\tfrac{1}{2}X_3) </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp= 3X_1+2.5X_2-0.5X_3 </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </task>
      <!-- <task>
        <statement>
   <p>
    We saw that we can express the <m>m</m>th principal components <m>Z_m</m> as the dot product
      <md>
        <mrow> Z_m \amp= \sum_{j=1}^{p}\phi_{jm}X_j=X_1 \phi_{1m}+X_2 \phi_{2m}+\ldots+X_p \phi_{pm}</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \begin{bmatrix}X_1 \amp X_2 \amp \ldots \amp X_p\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\phi_{1m}\\\phi_{2m}\\ \vdots\\\phi_{pm} \end{bmatrix}, </mrow>
      </md>
      and the PCR model as
      <md>
        <mrow>y_i \amp= \theta_0 + \sum_{m=1}^{M}\theta_m Z_{im} + \epsilon_i</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp= \begin{bmatrix}\theta_0 \amp \theta_1 \amp \ldots \amp \theta_M \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 \\ Z_{i1} \\ Z_{i2} \\ \vdots \\ Z_{iM} \end{bmatrix}. </mrow>
      </md>

      What is the vector-matrix product that will give <m>y_i</m> in terms of the original predictors <m>X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_p</m>?

      
    </p>
        </statement>
      </task> -->
    </example>

    <p>
      Conclusion:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            You cannot directly use the coefficients learned for the principal
            components to interpret the original inputs!
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Each PC is a linear combination of the original predictors, so you
            can work backward to determine the corresponding coefficients, as
            shown in <xref ref="day19-PCR-original-coeffs"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Assume we have three input variables <m>X_1, X_2, X_3</m> and two principal components (PCs), defined as:
            <md>
                <mrow>Z_1 \amp= 0.221 \cdot X_1 - 0.293 \cdot X_2 + 0.930 \cdot X_3</mrow>
                <mrow>Z_2 \amp= 0.677 \cdot X_1 - 0.640 \cdot X_2 - 0.362 \cdot X_3</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using linear regression, we learn the model 
                <md>Y = 2 + 4Z_1 - 3Z_2.</md> 
                What are the coefficients for the model learned in terms of the <m>X_i</m>'s? Show your work.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Substitute the equations for <m>Z_1</m> and <m>Z_2</m> into the model for <m>Y</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>Y \amp = 2 + 4(0.221 \cdot X_1 - 0.293 \cdot X_2 + 0.930 \cdot X_3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp \quad - 3(0.677 \cdot X_1 - 0.640 \cdot X_2 - 0.362 \cdot X_3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 2 - 1.147 X_1 + 0.748 X_2 + 4.806 X_3</mrow>
                </md>
                The final model coefficients are <m>-1.147</m> for <m>X_1</m>, <m>0.748</m> for <m>X_2</m>, and <m>4.806</m> for <m>X_3</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                You have a data point <m>(X_1, X_2, X_3) = (5, -2, 4)</m>. What would be the coordinates of this point in the PC space (also known as the scores)? Show your work.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Substitute the coordinates of the data point into the PC formulas:
                <md>
                    <mrow>Z_1 \amp = 0.221(5) - 0.293(-2) + 0.930(4)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 1.105 + 0.586 + 3.720 = 5.411</mrow>
                    <mrow>Z_2 \amp = 0.677(5) - 0.640(-2) - 0.362(4)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 3.385 + 1.280 - 1.448 = 3.217</mrow>
                </md>
                The coordinates in the PC space are <m>(5.411, 3.217)</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
</exercise>



  <paragraphs>
    <title>Picking <m>M</m></title>

    <image source="figures/Fig6-20.png">
      <shortdescription>
        Two plots showing standardized PCR coefficients (left) and
        cross-validation MSE (right) versus the number of principal components.
      </shortdescription>
    </image>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>Credit data set.</li>
        <li>
          There is a major drop in MSE at <m>M=10</m> components, so there is little
          savings compared with using the original <m>p=11</m> variables.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Bias-Variance Trade-off</title>

    <p>
      Example with simulated data: <m>n=50</m> observations of <m>p=45</m>
      predictors.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <m>Y</m> is a function of all predictors.
        </p>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-18a_with_legend.png" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
            Plot of training and test Mean Squared Error versus number of
            components, illustrating the bias-variance tradeoff.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Typical U shape for test MSE.</li>
            <li>
              Substantial improvement over least squares (least squares corresponds to rightmost value at <m>M=p=45</m> ).
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <m>Y</m> is a function of 2 predictors.
        </p>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-18b.png" width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
            Plot of squared bias, variance, and test MSE versus number of
            components, demonstrating the bias-variance decomposition.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Typical U shape for test MSE.</li>
            <li>
              Slight improvement over least squares (least squares corresponds to rightmost value at <m>M=p=45</m> ).
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Comparison to results on shrinkage</title>


        <p>
          <m>Y</m> is a function of all predictors.
        </p>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-18a.png" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>
            Plot of training and test Mean Squared Error versus number of
            components, illustrating the bias-variance tradeoff.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-8.png" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two plots showing MSE, squared bias, and variance versus lambda
            (left) and training R-squared (right), illustrating the
            bias-variance tradeoff.
          </shortdescription>

          <description>
            <p>
              Left: Plots of squared bias (black), variance (green), and test
              MSE (purple) for the lasso on a simulated data set.
              Right: Comparison of squared bias, variance, and test MSE between
              lasso (solid) and ridge (dotted).
              Both are plotted against their R2 on the training data, as a
              common form of indexing.
              The crosses in both plots indicate the lasso model for which the
              MSE is smallest
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>

        <p>
          <m>Y</m> is a function of 2 predictors.
        </p>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-18b.png" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>
            Plot of squared bias, variance, and test MSE versus number of
            components, demonstrating the bias-variance decomposition.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>

        <image source="figures/Fig6-9.png" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two plots showing MSE, squared bias, and variance for Lasso versus
            lambda (left) and training R-squared (right).
          </shortdescription>

          <description>
            <p>
              Left: Plots of squared bias (black), variance (green), and test
              MSE (purple) for the lasso.              
              Right: Comparison of squared bias, variance, and test MSE between
              lasso (solid) and ridge (dotted).
              Both are plotted against their R2 on the training data, as a
              common form of indexing.
              The crosses in both plots indicate the lasso model for which the
              MSE is smallest.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>


    <p>
      Shrinkage does better.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Properties of PCR</title>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Note that PCR performed better in the previous example because
              many PCs were needed.
            </li>

            <li>
              PCR performs better when the information is contained in the first
              few PCs.
            </li>

            <li>
              The number of PCs to use can be selected with cross-validation
              (CV).
            </li>

            <li>
              Standardization is needed to ensure that large data values do not
              skew the results.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>

      <stack>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              PCR is not a feature selection method because it does not produce
              a subset of features; instead, it produces a collection of linear
              combinations of features.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </stack>
    </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>
